LESSONS_P5 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The Purchase Order: scope and position", "11", "<p>This module covers the Purchase Order in ZhiftERP. On completing this module you will be able to: complete the Purchase Order field walk with the demand trace and the sourced rates; state what submission commits and what it updates; structure multi-drop delivery schedules; operate the amendment and cancellation discipline; configure the purchase tax side and handle supplier advances; and run the order book reads.</p><p><b>What a Purchase Order is.</b> The Purchase Order is the buying commitment: the business commits to buy these items, in these quantities, at these rates, delivered on this schedule, on these terms. Everything before it prepared the decision — the demand recorded and screened (module 3), the rates established (module 4); everything after it executes: the receipts arrive against it (module 6), the invoices match against it (module 7), and the supplier's performance measures against it (module 8). The order is the document both parties are held to, and its correctness at submission is what makes every downstream control a comparison rather than an investigation.</p><p><b>Order sources.</b></p><p>1. <b>From consolidated demand</b> — the standing path: the order creates against module 3's worksheet, drawing the Material Requests so the trace links automatically and %ordered advances on every source request.<br>2. <b>From an arrangement</b> — repeat lines inside a standing arrangement's validity: the demand still traces; the rates draw from the arrangement without a new cycle (module 4's exception operating as designed).<br>3. <b>The expedite lane</b> — the genuine emergency, with its named approval and its premium visible, per module 3's window discipline.</p><p><b>What submission does, stated in advance.</b> Chapter 3 details it: the ordered quantities enter the incoming figures the stock system carries (projected quantity rises), the receiving and matching machinery gains its basis, and the supplier is engaged on a document. An unsubmitted draft engages nobody; a submitted order commits the business's money and the supplier's capacity.</p><p><b>Custody.</b> The procurement function prepares orders; approval per the matrix's bands reviews the commitment — supplier, rate, terms, schedule — the need having been settled at the screen (module 3's separation); rate changes after submission re-enter the award governance; and the standing rule: <b>the order states the whole purchase — its demand, its rates' sources, its schedule, and its terms — and nothing about the purchase changes without its documented amendment.</b></p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the field walk. Chapter 3: submission effects. Chapter 4: the delivery schedule. Chapter 5: amendments and cancellations. Chapter 6: taxes and the money side. Chapter 7: the order book reads. Chapter 8: worked example. Chapter 9: review and the module rule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Purchase Order doctype", "12", "<p>Create the Purchase Order from its source — the Material Requests or the consolidation view — so the trace carries. Complete or verify the fields in order.</p><p><b>1. Supplier and the header pre-fill.</b> The supplier selection pulls module 1's chain: the billing currency, the payment terms template, the addresses and contact. The header is read before the rows — an order in the wrong currency is wrong on every figure.</p><p><b>2. The items table.</b> Per row:</p><p>1. <b>Item, quantity, unit</b> — from the consolidated demand, in the purchase units, the pairing rule read on every row (the quantity-unit-rate check from module 2 applies at order preparation as its first natural checkpoint).<br>2. <b>Rate</b> — from the buying list or arrangement, carrying its source (module 4's award); a rate differing from the sourced figure is a question answered before submission, not a field adjusted to proceed.<br>3. <b>Required By (per row)</b> — the schedule field: each row carries its delivery date, which is how one order states a multi-drop schedule (Chapter 4). The dates come from the demand's date structure on the worksheet.<br>4. <b>Warehouse (per row)</b> — where each row's goods should arrive: the hub in the standing pattern; split destinations where the arrangement delivers direct.<br>5. <b>The Material Request links</b> — carried by creation from the source: the trace that advances %ordered and answers the audit.</p><p><b>3. Taxes and Charges.</b> The purchase taxes template per the context (Chapter 6): VAT as the supplier will charge it, so the order's total states the commitment as it will be invoiced.</p><p><b>4. Terms and references.</b> The payment terms template from the supplier record (the negotiated 30 days computing the eventual invoice's due date); the arrangement or quotation reference; the supplier's own reference where one exists.</p><p><b>5. Save, review, submit.</b> Draft review per the value band — the approver reviews the commitment: the supplier against the award, the rates against their sources, the schedule against the demand's dates, the total against the band. Submission commits (Chapter 3) and the order transmits to the supplier's sales contact (module 1's named person) per the site's channel, with the transmission recorded.</p><p><b>6. The supplier's confirmation.</b> The order asks for confirmation: the supplier's acknowledgment of quantities, rates, and — critically — the delivery dates. The confirmation (or the silence the chase read surfaces) files on the order. An unconfirmed schedule is a hope; the confirmation converts it into the supplier's stated commitment, which is what module 8's on-time measurement runs against.</p><p><b>Verification per order.</b> Header currency and terms; rows paired and sourced; per-row dates from the demand; warehouses stated; taxes per template; the trace present; then submit, transmit, and obtain confirmation.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Submission effects", "12", "<p>Submission converts the draft into a commitment. This chapter states each effect and its consequence.</p><p><b>1. The incoming figures.</b> Each submitted row's undelivered quantity enters the item-warehouse's <b>ordered quantity</b>: projected quantity — actual minus reserved plus incoming — rises by the order. The consequences:</p><p>1. <b>The reorder machinery sees the pipeline</b> — the auto-trigger (module 3) computes on projected quantity, so a submitted order stops the same shortfall re-triggering: the engine does not order twice for one gap, provided orders submit promptly. A consolidation decided but not committed leaves the trigger armed — one of the reasons the buying window closes with orders submitted, not with worksheets approved.<br>2. <b>Availability answers include the pipeline</b> — the selling side's scheduling against stock (the Selling track, module 4) reads projected figures that now include this order and its dates.</p><p><b>2. The execution basis.</b> The submitted order becomes the reference the downstream documents draw from:</p><p>1. <b>Purchase Receipts</b> create against the order (module 6): quantities receive against ordered rows, and <b>%received</b> tracks fulfilment per row.<br>2. <b>Purchase Invoices</b> match against the order and its receipts (module 7): <b>%billed</b> tracks the claim, and the three-way comparison — ordered, received, billed — is the matching control's shape.<br>3. The two percentages state the order's life; the gaps between them are Chapter 7's reads.</p><p><b>3. The supplier's commercial position.</b> The transmitted order binds both parties on its terms: the rates and quantities per the award, the schedule per the rows, the terms per the template. From confirmation, schedule movements are events with owners — the supplier's delay is a chase-ladder entry and a scorecard fact (module 8); the business's own change is an amendment (Chapter 5).</p><p><b>4. What submission does not do.</b> It moves no stock (receipts do), creates no liability (invoices do), and pays nothing (payment entries do, on the invoice's due date). The order is the commitment; the downstream documents are the performance — the same promise-versus-performance separation as the selling side, and the reason the matching control in module 7 has three distinct documents to compare.</p><p><b>5. The commitment value.</b> The order book's total (Chapter 7) is money the business has committed but not yet spent: the owner's forward view of purchasing reads it weekly, and the cash planning (the payables due profile from module 1's summary) extends it — committed orders become invoices become due dates, and the three views read together are the buying side's cash forecast.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — The delivery schedule", "12", "<p>Real purchases arrive in drops. This chapter is the scheduling machinery on the buying side.</p><p><b>1. Per-row schedule dates.</b> The items table carries Required By per row — the mechanism for every schedule shape:</p><p>1. <b>The single-drop order</b> — all rows on one date.<br>2. <b>The multi-drop order</b> — the same item split across rows with different dates: the 300-carton purchase entered as two rows — 180 on the first date, 120 on the second — each row receiving independently, %received advancing per drop. The split-row structure is the standing method because each drop is then its own schedule commitment, chased and measured on its own date.<br>3. <b>The rule:</b> the rows state the schedule the demand's dates require and the supplier confirmed — the same real-plan rule as the selling side, because every planning read below computes from these dates.</p><p><b>2. Why multi-drop.</b> The structure serves both parties and is priced knowingly:</p><p>1. <b>The business's side</b> — stock arrives as the cover dates require rather than all at once: warehouse capacity, shelf-life freshness on dated goods (later drops manufactured later), and cash timing (invoicing commonly follows delivery, so drops spread the payable).<br>2. <b>The supplier's side</b> — production and logistics scheduling against confirmed dates.<br>3. <b>The volume term holds across the order</b> — the arrangement's band prices the order's total quantity, not each drop: 300 cartons at the band rate, delivered 180 and 120, is one commercial commitment on one order — which is what makes the multi-drop structure superior to two separate orders that would each miss the band.</p><p><b>3. The incoming planning read — weekly.</b> Open order rows by required-by date:</p><p>1. <b>The coming window</b> — rows due in the horizon, grouped by warehouse: the receiving workload (module 6's dock plans from it) and the stock arrivals the selling side's availability reads.<br>2. <b>The overdue rows</b> — rows past date and unreceived: each chased per the ladder below.<br>3. <b>The at-risk rows</b> — confirmed dates the supplier has signalled against, or unconfirmed rows inside their lead time: flagged while alternatives (the split to the secondary supplier, the expedite) are still open.</p><p><b>4. The chase ladder.</b> Overdue incoming runs a stepped procedure, mirroring the collections gears in structure: the query at the date (the row is due — where is it), the escalation at the interval (the supplier's named contact, then the relationship level), and the sourcing decision where the delay breaks the cover (the secondary supplier's share activating per the split award, the expedite lane, or the customer-side conversation where a sales commitment depends on it). Every step records on the order — the delay's history is module 8's on-time evidence, per drop, per date.</p><p><b>5. Schedule changes.</b> Date movements are amendments (Chapter 5) with their party recorded: the supplier's slip is their event (chased and scored); the business's deferral is its own (communicated and documented). The schedule's history then states what was committed, what moved, whose movement it was — the record the module 8 review and any commercial dispute reads.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Amendments and cancellations", "12", "<p>Commitments change; the record must change correctly. This chapter is the buying-side discipline.</p><p><b>1. The change taxonomy.</b> Changes route by what they touch:</p><p>1. <b>Rate changes</b> — a rate on a submitted order moves only within the award's governance: the arrangement's dated amendment (module 4, Chapter 6), or a re-award for material changes. A supplier's request to invoice above the order's rate is a matching exception (module 7), not an order edit to make the mismatch disappear — the order states the agreed rate precisely so the invoice can be compared to it.<br>2. <b>Quantity changes</b> — increases are new demand: screened (module 3) and approved per the band, because an order amendment is not a route around the screen. Decreases release incoming (projected quantity falls — the reorder engine may re-trigger, correctly) and are checked against the supplier's position per the arrangement's terms (a band-priced order reduced below its band is a rate conversation, stated before the amendment, not discovered at the invoice).<br>3. <b>Schedule changes</b> — per Chapter 4.5: the movement, its party, its reason, the confirmation updated.<br>4. <b>Administrative changes</b> — references and contacts: ordinary edits with notes.</p><p><b>2. The amendment mechanics.</b> The controlled lanes:</p><p>1. <b>The update facility for submitted orders</b> — quantity and schedule updates on unreceived rows, role-limited, system-logged: the standard lane.<br>2. <b>Cancel-and-amend</b> — structural changes: the order cancels with reason and the amended version carries the reference; receipts already posted gate what cancellation is possible, and an order with received drops amends around its performed portion.<br>3. <b>Either lane:</b> the change, the reason, the approver per the taxonomy, the supplier's agreement where the commercial position moved — on the document trail.</p><p><b>3. Cancellations.</b> The order that will not proceed cancels with its reason code (demand ceased, supplier failure, superseded by re-award), releasing its incoming figures and closing its schedule rows. The supplier is informed formally — a cancelled order the supplier still manufactures against is a dispute in preparation — and the cancellation's cause files where it belongs: supplier-failure cancellations are scorecard facts; demand-ceased cancellations route back to module 3's pipeline hygiene.</p><p><b>4. The Closed status.</b> The deliberate early end: the remainder will not be taken, incoming releases, the record preserves what happened — used at the stale-order read (Chapter 7) with its reason, per the same rule as every early closure in the programme.</p><p><b>5. The amendments read — monthly.</b> Orders amended in the month: the change, the lane, the approver, reconciled against the taxonomy — rate changes carrying award governance, quantity increases carrying screens, schedule changes carrying parties and confirmations. The buying side's fields-move-by-decision audit, filed with the month's records.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Taxes and the money side", "12", "<p>The order carries the purchase's tax structure and, where terms require, its advance. This chapter is the money side before the invoice.</p><p><b>1. Purchase Taxes and Charges.</b> The buying mirror of the selling template: ordered rows, each a charge with its type, account head, and rate — maintained by the accountant, selected on documents:</p><p>1. <b>The VAT row</b> — the supplier's VAT as it will be charged: on standard-rated purchases, 7.5% on the net, with the account head per the accountant's input-VAT design. The order's total then states the commitment as the invoice will state the claim, and the matching comparison (module 7) includes the tax arithmetic.<br>2. <b>Item-level treatments</b> — exempt purchases (the pharmaceutical classes on the buying side) compute per line through the item tax templates, exactly as the selling side: one document, mixed treatments, no hand edits.<br>3. <b>The recoverability question is the accountant's</b> — input VAT's treatment in the return (recoverable against output, or restricted per the class) is ruled and configured on the accounts side; the procurement obligation is documents whose tax rows are computed by template and therefore usable by the return.</p><p><b>2. Withholding, located.</b> Withholding tax applies at payment against the supplier's ruling (module 1's field) — it is a payment-time deduction, not an order-time charge: the order and invoice state the supplier's full claim; the payment entry withholds per the ruling and remits per the calendar. The location matters because withholding entered as an order discount misstates both the commitment and the statutory record.</p><p><b>3. Supplier advances.</b> Where the arrangement's terms require payment before delivery (import lines commonly do; the domestic 30-day terms do not):</p><p>1. <b>The advance is the terms' row</b> — stated on the order's payment schedule as negotiated, per the same schedule machinery as the selling side.<br>2. <b>The Payment Entry records it against the Purchase Order</b> — the advance held against the supplier, visible on the ledger.<br>3. <b>The allocation completes at invoicing</b> — the advance applies against the invoice per the schedule; the unallocated-advances read (the payables mirror of the selling side's weekly read) lists advances without allocations to zero.<br>4. <b>The exposure is real</b> — an advance is the business's money in the supplier's hands before goods exist: the policy bands advance approvals accordingly, and the advance-exposure read lists outstanding advances by supplier with ages, because a supplier holding aged advances is a risk figure the review reads.</p><p><b>4. The commitment's currency.</b> Foreign-currency orders carry the exposure module 1 stated: the order commits in the supplier's currency; the naira value moves until payment. The order file notes the exposure window per module 2's worksheet, and the accountant's revaluation machinery handles the ledger — the procurement contribution is, as everywhere, honest documents in true currencies.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The order book reads", "12", "<p>The open orders are the business's committed inbound supply. This chapter is the reads that keep the book true and worked.</p><p><b>1. The order book — weekly.</b> All open orders with value, age, %received, %billed, and next scheduled date: the committed-money view (Chapter 3.5) and the working list, passed top-down by value with the largest commitments' next dates confirmed.</p><p><b>2. The incoming planning read — weekly (Chapter 4.3, restated as the book's column).</b> Due, overdue, and at-risk rows, each with its ladder step and owner. The read's output feeds three consumers: the receiving dock's workload (module 6), the selling side's availability picture, and the chase ladder's worklist.</p><p><b>3. The received-not-billed read — weekly.</b> Receipts whose invoices have not arrived or posted within the cadence: goods the business holds against a claim not yet stated. The list is worked two ways — the supplier chased for the invoice (the payables position cannot be planned from missing claims), and the accrual side handed to the accountant at period end per the Accounts track's procedures. Module 7 owns the matching; this read is its feeder.</p><p><b>4. The billed-not-received read — weekly.</b> The inverse: invoices claiming goods the dock has not received. Legitimate cases trace to their structure (the advance invoice per the arrangement's schedule); the remainder are matching exceptions worked before payment — the read exists because paying claims ahead of goods is the exposure the three-way match guards, and module 7 details the control.</p><p><b>5. The stale-order hygiene — monthly.</b> Open orders with no movement across the policy window: each revived with a confirmed schedule (the supplier re-commits dates by amendment) or Closed with its reason, releasing incoming. The cost of staleness is stated plainly: a stale order holds incoming quantity that suppresses the reorder engine — the trigger that should fire does not, because projected quantity includes an arrival that will never come. The hygiene read is therefore a stock-out prevention, not bookkeeping.</p><p><b>6. The cancellations and closures read — quarterly.</b> The quarter's cancelled and closed orders by reason code: supplier-failure concentrations routed to the module 8 review and the split-award decisions; demand-ceased patterns routed to module 3's screening quality. Data with names, per the standing rule.</p><p><b>7. The filing.</b> The order file per order — the demand trace, the award reference, the confirmation, the amendments, the drops, the receipts and invoices as they arrive — accumulates to the one-lookup standard, and modules 6-7 write the performance onto it.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Worked example: the 300-carton Emzor order", "13", "<p>This chapter commits the purchase modules 3-4 prepared. Values and outcomes are listed per step.</p><p><b>Step 1 — creation from the consolidation (Chapter 2).</b> The Purchase Order creates against the consolidated demand: supplier <b>Emzor</b> (the header pulling naira, <b>30-day terms</b>, the named sales contact); one item — PARA-500 — structured as <b>two rows for the two drops: 180 cartons required next week, 120 cartons the week after</b>, both rows to Central Warehouse; the rate <b>₦9,600 per Carton-12</b> drawing from the arrangement, its source the module 4 decision minute. The Material Request links carry: the Central trigger's 260, Ibadan's 15, Akure's 25 — %ordered advances on all three at submission.</p><p><b>Step 2 — the pairing and the band confirmed (Chapters 2, 4).</b> The pre-submission verification reads the rows: quantity-unit-rate paired (cartons against the carton rate throughout); the two-row total of 300 confirmed at the arrangement's volume band — one order, one commercial commitment, the band rate holding across both drops, which two separate 180- and 120-carton orders would each have missed.</p><p><b>Step 3 — submission and its effects (Chapter 3).</b> The taxes compute per the purchase template (the exempt pharmaceutical class per its item treatment); the approval reviews the commitment against the award and the band; submission enters 300 cartons into Central's incoming — projected quantity rises above the reorder level, and the auto-trigger stands down: the engine sees the pipeline and does not order twice for one gap. The order transmits to Emzor's sales contact; the confirmation returns accepting quantities, the band rate, and <b>both drop dates</b> — the schedule is now the supplier's stated commitment, filed on the order.</p><p><b>Step 4 — the book carries it (Chapter 7).</b> The weekly order book shows the commitment at value with its two dates; the incoming planning read places drop one in the coming window — the receiving dock (module 6) plans for 180 cartons; the selling side's availability picture includes the arrival. %received and %billed sit at zero, ready to advance per drop.</p><p><b>Step 5 — the money side, stated (Chapter 6).</b> No advance applies — the 30-day terms mean the invoice for each drop will fall due thirty days after its date, and the payables due profile extends accordingly: the two drops spread the payable exactly as the multi-drop structure intends. Withholding does not apply to the goods class per the ruling; the input-VAT side is the exempt class's configured treatment — the money side of this order is therefore entirely the matching and payment machinery modules 6-7 will execute.</p><p><b>The handoff.</b> The order file stands complete: three requests, the worksheet, the award minute, the order with two confirmed drops, the trace live in both directions. Drop one — <b>180 cartons, next week</b> — is module 6's receiving case, where the dock discipline, the identity capture, and the 177-sound-3-crushed receipt await.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common errors and the module rule", "11", "<p>The errors that recur in Purchase Orders, each with its correction and prevention.</p><p><b>Error 1 — the order without its demand.</b> Symptom: orders raised from conversations; no requests behind the rows; the trace audit finding orphans. Correction: the demand recorded and screened retroactively where genuine. Prevention: creation from the consolidation or requests, and the gate configuration where the site enforces it.</p><p><b>Error 2 — the adjusted rate.</b> Symptom: order rates edited away from their sources to match a supplier's ask or an invoice's claim; the award governance bypassed; matching comparing a document to itself. Correction: the rate restored to its source; the change routed through the arrangement's amendment or the matching exception. Prevention: rates carry sources, and differences are questions answered before submission.</p><p><b>Error 3 — the unconfirmed schedule.</b> Symptom: orders transmitted into silence; dates the supplier never committed to; on-time measurement against hopes. Correction: confirmations chased through the read. Prevention: confirmation as part of the order procedure, and the unconfirmed-rows flag in the planning read.</p><p><b>Error 4 — the late submission.</b> Symptom: consolidations decided but orders unsubmitted; the reorder trigger still armed and firing again; double demand in the pipeline. Correction: the duplicate demand stopped; the window's discipline restored. Prevention: the buying window closes with orders submitted, not worksheets approved.</p><p><b>Error 5 — the stale order suppressing the trigger.</b> Symptom: incoming quantity from orders that will never arrive; projected figures above reorder levels; the stock-out arriving with the trigger silent. Correction: the hygiene read — revive with confirmed dates or Close with reason. Prevention: the monthly stale-order read, treated as stock-out prevention.</p><p><b>Error 6 — withholding as a discount.</b> Symptom: withholding deducted on the order or invoice figures; the commitment and the statutory record both misstated. Correction: the documents restated at full claim; withholding relocated to payment per the ruling. Prevention: the location rule — orders and invoices state the full claim; payment withholds.</p><p><b>Review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: complete the field walk with the trace, sourced rates, per-row dates, and warehouses; state submission's effects on projected quantity and the execution basis, and what submission does not do; structure multi-drop schedules with the band-across-the-order rule and run the chase ladder; route changes by the taxonomy through the controlled lanes; configure the purchase tax side, locate withholding at payment, and manage advances with their exposure read; and run the order book's six reads.</p><p><b>Module rule 5: the Purchase Order states the whole purchase — created from screened demand at sourced rates, scheduled per row as the confirmed plan, committing the incoming figures at submission, changed only through the taxonomy's lanes, its taxes computed and its advances controlled — and kept live by the book's reads so the trigger, the dock, and the match all compute from truth.</b> Module 6 covers Purchase Receipts.</p>"),
]

